so this is not the type of story I would  normally talk about but it is kind of a  perfect mix of pop culture and current  events with the ongoing gun violence  issues that we have in the United States  so I do want to take a few minutes to  talk about it a group of people showed  up at a Popeye's fast-food restaurant  and demanded fried chicken sandwiches at  gunpoint this was in Houston Texas and  to understand this you need to  understand what's been going on with  Popeyes and chicken sandwiches the whole  backstory is that Popeyes introduced the  chicken sandwich offering and they did  it with a very well calculated viral  marketing campaign and it became a  complete social media sensation and  while I haven't been to a fast-food  restaurant in more than 15 years at this  point I was still following it as  something interesting from a social and  business and marketing perspective and  these sandwiches went crazy these  sandwiches at Popeyes sold out  nationwide and they've been sold out for  some time now it was a three dollar  99-cent chicken sandwich I believe  people went crazy for this sandwich and  they ran out of ingredients and now  they've said we don't know when it'll be  back and we're trying to source what we  need for it and the genius of the  campaign in part was that Popeyes social  media campaign sort of went after  chick-fil-a a rival chicken chain  tongue-in-cheek but very very effective  Wendy's jumped in on it as well I don't  care about this food I never go to these  places but it was clever and it worked  all over the countries the sandwiches  are just totally sold out so in Houston  Texas not shockingly shortly before 9  p.m. three men and two women show up  with at least one firearm they had  already gone through the drive-thru they  had already been told the store was out  of chicken sandwiches and they tried to  go inside with their firearm to demand  chicken sandwiches one report says that  an employee locked the door before  anybody got in another report says the  people did get in so I'm unclear as to  which version of that detail is accurate  the group reportedly had a baby with  them but the baby was left in the car  when they tried to use a weapon to force  the store to give them chicken  sandwiches police as of this morning  are looking for the suspects maybe by  the time you hear this or watch this the  suspects will have been caught now I  know I say this all the time we have  a problem in the United States with  people who see guns as a tool to solve  problems I get that this is chicken  sandwiches but it's the same damn  pattern I've been pointing out for years  in the United States yes we have too  many guns yes it's too easy to get guns  we lack basic gun safety regulations  ammunition should be more expensive all  of that stuff is true it all needs to be  dealt with but we also have a cultural  problem with guns and one piece of that  problem is that people think of firearms  as ways to solve problems or to get  things personal problems in the United  States too many people see as possibly  being solved with a gun political  problems people see is possibly being  solved with a gun cartoonishly here a  popular sold out chicken sandwich  availability problem is seen as  potentially being solvable with a gun  now of course the poor decision-making  skills by these individuals are  particularly to blame in this particular  case but there are people who want to  ignore the broader context that as silly  as trying to get sold out chicken  sandwiches with a gun is it's happening  in a country where people try to solve  all sorts of problems with guns and to  pretend that that is not the context is  deceptive it's incomplete and it is not  going to further an understanding of or  a solution to what it is that we are  currently dealing with here in any case  I hope that they find these people as of  right now they are still looking and no  even this is not going to get me back to  fast food I think I'm actually trying to  think I've said 15 years but I'm really  kind of getting up there now I think  it's been more like 18 years yeah you  know there's one exception when a KFC  opened in a place I was living when I  was in my early 20s I think I did once  or twice go over to KFC as I recently  said on the bonus show and I got one of  those crispy strip meals with the mashed  potatoes and that great coleslaw that  KFC has I'm sure it's terrible for you  but it tastes great but other than those  two slip-ups at KFC it's been like 17 or  18 years  since I've really been to a fast-food  restaurant anyway I don't know you've  tried the sandwich let me know if it's  actually that that good
 

